Oh, as far as I remember, Jonadab's suggestion was "writing [with my
marker] instead of wishing" to get the scroll, not "wishing for marker
instead of wishing for scroll". My intention was to not wish at all,
unless necessary. I had a marker and I needed the marker charges for
identification; this had been top priority. So I decided to not gamble
with my few valuable marker charges. And I had no need to use a wish,
neither for the scroll, nor for a marker.

Of course, with a lot of wishes one may decide to just spend the wishes
for something helpful. But that's not the way I play. I do wait until I
see a necessity to spend the wishes.

> elsewhere, that's fine, and I won't argue that point.  but if you get to
> the late game with an extra wish and you have everything you need except
> your weapon is +0, it would be better to spend the wish on a magic marker
> than on scrolls of enchant weapon.

My play is probably different. Usually I have a lot of spare wishes; my
last game was wishless, and I had a wand of wishing (0:2). In the Rogue
game which was the base for this thread I used 6 wishes, but I still had
a wand of wishing (1:3), a wand of wishing (1:0), and a magic lamp.

I've anyway found the scroll just in time, as reported. If that would not
have been the case the question would have been, what is better; wishing
for a marker and writing an unknown scroll (and possibly not gaining the
scroll at all in that process!), or wishing for scrolls once (to be sure
you can write them later), and use an existing marker (or another marker
from one of the spare wishes) to reliably write many more scrolls without
wasting charges like in the first case.

The decision is crucial only in cases (unrelated to the current context)
where you have just one shot and need the desired object. In such cases
what would you do? I understand you prefer wishing for the marker; that's
not unreasonable. But what if the RNG fools you and your plan doesn't work
out? You may stay with valuable resources spent and nothing got. You know
the odds and decide to go that way, that's fine; you are fairly successful
and it works for you. Since my own experiences with the RNG are different,
I suppose, from yours I don't prefer that gambling; and I am also fairly
successful. My intention is not to convince anyone. But calling abstinence
from this sort of "gambling" silly, as you've did, is plain nonsense.

> 
> There are times when attempting to write an unknown scroll is a good
> gamble.  Removing this tool from your toolbox is not intelligent resource
> management.

No one suggested that you should remove anything from your toolbox.

But intelligent resource management starts _before_ the decision whether
to wish for A or for B; it starts with the decision whether it's necessary
to spend a wish or not.

